# The address of a call is no statement and declares nothing. Read by its
# shape, before the calls are bound, `@: f(x b: 1)` is a pointer to f named x.
# The binding then resolves f(x b: 1) as a call, and the reader of
# declarations answers by that role whether or not it kept the earlier
# reading: the statement is refused as a body that is no declaration. Answered
# from the kept reading, the refusal would name f an unknown type at column 8.
fn: f (int: a; int: b) int
    return: a * 2 + b
end: f

fn: use (int: x) int
    @: f(x b: 1)
    return: x
end: use

sendMessage: exit(exit_code: use(3); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
